I've read the roster. Key reading decisions before the plan:

**Times:** the header says *"All times are LOCAL to the station shown"* — so I've converted everything myself. September 2026 is still summer time: **LHR/DUB = UTC+1 (BST/IST)**, **ARN/BCN/FRA/CDG/FCO = UTC+2 (CEST)**. Every instant below is UTC. Reporting offset applied is **40 min** (all sectors are short-haul on the AT72); the printed "Off Duty" column is STA + 30 min, which matches every line, so the duty lengths check out.

**Shape of the month:** 13 days off, 2 home standbys, no nightstops — it's all out-and-back LHR flying. The fatigue problem isn't timezones, it's the **whiplash between 05:45 reports and 02:00–03:00 finishes**, with an 85-minute commute bolted onto both ends. Report at 05:45 means an 03:30 alarm; a 03:05 off-duty means walking in your front door at 04:30. Several of your "days off" (13th, 16th, 20th, 24th) are sandwiched between those two extremes and are not really days off at all — I've split sleep on those days rather than pretend a normal night is available.

